---
import { sitePath } from '../data/site';
import Guide from '../layouts/Guide.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
---
<Guide title="Record TLS. Replay the application." description="Record supported TLS applications into one PCAPNG, then replay recovered messages through fresh verified connections. No separate key-log file to manage." sections={[{id:'record',label:'Record one replayable file'},{id:'tls',label:'Connect from a capture'},{id:'application',label:'Replay application data'},{id:'identity',label:'Verify the target'},{id:'ftps',label:'FTPS'},{id:'ssh',label:'SSH'},{id:'limits',label:'Recovery limits'}]}>
  <div class="notice"><strong>Keep the recording and its secrets local.</strong>A PCAPNG can contain embedded TLS secrets. Treat it and any matching key log as sensitive. This website never accepts captures, keys, or passwords.</div>
  <h2 id="record">One recording. Fresh application replay.</h2>
  <p>Launch a supported TLS application through Livewire. It records the traffic, collects that application's exported session secrets, and embeds only matching replay secrets in one PCAPNG. Later, <code>live</code> recovers the recorded requests and plays them through a fresh verified TLS connection.</p>
  <Code code={"livewire capture -i <interface> -o issue.pcapng -tls -- <application> [args...]\nlivewire live issue.pcapng -t device.example:1502"} label="Record and replay TLS application traffic" />
  <p>No separate key-log file or HTTP request file is needed. Supported application replay includes Modbus, DNS/TCP, MQTT and DNP3 carried over TLS, as well as HTTP/1. Use <code>check -details</code> to inspect and select the exchange.</p>
  <div class="notice"><strong>The original application must support key export.</strong>Livewire supplies a private <code>SSLKEYLOGFILE</code> to the launched process. Python clients using <code>ssl.create_default_context()</code> and compatible curl/browser builds can export keys; other applications need their TLS library's key-export integration. An old encrypted-only capture cannot gain missing secrets retroactively.</div>
  <p>The application must run in the foreground and finish normally. Recording needs packet-capture privileges; fresh application replay does not. Interruption or failed key collection returns a nonzero result and preserves an explicitly partial capture. The temporary log is cleaned up, while the PCAPNG remains sensitive. <a href={sitePath("/reference/tls-capture/#record-tls-applications-into-one-file")}>See client compatibility and recording limits →</a></p>
  <h2 id="tls">Start a fresh TLS connection</h2><Code code={"livewire live tls.pcap -t device.example:443"} label="Fresh TLS from a capture" /><p>No external key log or HTTP request file is required to perform a handshake. With a complete supported ClientHello, Livewire reads public SNI, ALPN, and version offers, then opens a fresh TCP connection and verified TLS session to your target.</p><p>Without matching secrets, the attempt sends no application bytes. It can expose negotiation, trust, ALPN, and connection failures. It cannot recover or resend the encrypted application exchange.</p><div class="notice"><strong>Handshake complete. Application replay incomplete.</strong>A successful handshake is reported separately. Application completion, response verification, and response matching remain false, with zero application comparisons. <code>-strict-exit</code> returns failure for this incomplete replay.</div>
  <h2 id="application">Replay the captured application exchange</h2><p>Matching secrets let Livewire recover recorded application messages and use a supported adapter over a fresh verified TLS session. Choose the input your recording provides:</p><div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="TLS capture inputs"><table><thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>What can run</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ClientHello without secrets</td><td>A fresh TLS handshake; application replay stays incomplete.</td></tr><tr><td>PCAPNG with matching embedded TLSK secrets</td><td>Captured application replay without an external key-log file.</td></tr><tr><td>Capture plus explicit <code>-keylog</code></td><td>Captured application replay using that file; it takes priority over embedded secrets.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><Code code={"livewire live tls-with-secrets.pcapng -t device.example:443\nlivewire live tls.pcap -keylog sslkeys.log -t device.example:443"} label="Embedded secrets or an explicit matching key log" /><p>Embedded secrets are still secrets. They are read from the capture snapshot in memory and excluded from reports. Malformed or conflicting secrets, missing session keys, and decryption errors stop before sending; they do not silently select the handshake-only path.</p><p>A fresh connection produces new keys and transport state. Supported adapters can account for live application identifiers; this does not reproduce the original encrypted packets or guarantee that the device issue recurs.</p>
  <p>Modbus/TCP over server-authenticated TLS can replay recovered Modbus messages on a fresh connection. Encrypted-only captures cannot reveal the recorded register operations. TLS client-certificate authentication (mTLS) and full Modbus Security are not supported.</p>
  <h2 id="identity">Verify the live target</h2><p>Certificate verification is on by default. Handshake-only replay uses captured SNI when available, otherwise the target hostname or IP. Use your lab CA and an explicit expected server name when needed.</p><Code code={"livewire live tls.pcap -t 192.0.2.50:443 -ca device-ca.pem -server-name device.example"} label="Private CA · explicit verified identity" /><p>Resolve certificate errors by checking the hostname, CA, certificate validity, and device clock. Keep verification enabled. Use <code>-timeout</code> to bound the connection and handshake, and <code>-session</code> to select an exchange from a mixed capture.</p>
  <h2 id="ftps">FTPS includes the data connection</h2><p>FTP and explicit or implicit FTPS use a coordinated control/data workflow. Secure replay needs matching secrets and recoverable transfer data. Supported protection transitions and transfer evidence are checked; missing data or unsupported security stops the operation. FTPS does not inherit the direct TLS handshake-only fallback.</p><Code code={"livewire live ftps.pcapng -t ftp.example:21 -ca lab-ca.pem -server-name ftp.example"} label="Recorded FTPS · embedded secrets" /><p>Recording a supported FTPS client collects secrets for its protected control and data connections, including active transfers where the server opens TCP. Keep both streams together and include both in any session selection. Provide fresh FTP credentials when needed. A successful control login alone does not establish that a file transfer completed.</p>
  <h2 id="ssh">SSH needs explicit operations</h2><p>An SSH capture cannot supply plaintext commands. Provide credentials, the expected host key, and the operations you intend to run. Prefer a dedicated lab key.</p><Code code={"livewire live ssh.pcap -t device.example:22 -user lab -key id_ed25519 -host-key device_host_key.pub -cmd \"show version\" -expect \"Version\""} label="Fresh SSH · pinned host key" /><p>Unified SSH replay requires a pinned host key. Reports retain lengths and hashes instead of command text and response bodies.</p>
  <h2 id="limits">Know the replay boundary</h2><ul><li>Handshake-only replay filters captured offers to TLS 1.2/1.3. Old-only offers, ECH, malformed or incomplete ClientHello, and TCP gaps are refused before dialing.</li><li>Exact ClientHello fingerprints, cipher offers, captured timing, session resumption, PSKs, and early data are not reproduced.</li><li>Unknown inner protocols and unsupported authentication are not silently downgraded to ordinary TCP.</li><li>Application fault checks, scenarios, timing, and durable resume require application replay with matching secrets.</li><li>Captured SSH ciphertext cannot be converted into commands. DNP3 Secure Authentication and MQTT enhanced authentication are outside the supported automatic replay path.</li></ul><p class="reference-link"><a href={sitePath("/reference/tls-capture/")}>Read the complete TLS capture guide →</a> · <a href={sitePath("/reference/reliability/")}>Protocol and security limits →</a></p>
</Guide>
